====================================================================================================
12. END-TO-END dephasing normalization on free precession (the load-bearing regression)
====================================================================================================
  Gaussian S_b(omega) = sqrt(2 pi) (dbz^2/sigma) exp[-omega^2/(2 sigma^2)] with sigma = 2.0 and
  dbz = rms of the sigma_z COEFFICIENT = 0.3; tau = 1; F = 4 sin^2(omega tau/2)
    C(0) = (1/2 pi) int S_b d omega  = 0.09  (= dbz^2 = 0.09)
    <a_1^2> = (1/pi) int_0^inf S_b F/omega^2 = 0.06875600894468
    direct double integral of C(t1-t2) = 0.06875600894468   (agreement 0.0)
    W = exp(-2<a_1^2>) = 0.871523876057   [the printed exp(-<a_1^2>) = 0.933554431223 UNDER-predicts the exponent by 2x]
    F_av = (1 + W)/2 = 0.935761938028;  1 - F_av = 0.0642380619717 against the linear <a_1^2> = 0.0687560089447
    Biercuk chi = (2/pi) int_0^inf S_b F/omega^2 = 0.137512017889 = 2<a_1^2> exactly
    reading S_beta as the SPLITTING-fluctuation PSD (4 S_b) instead: 0.550048071557, a factor 4 too large
    matched-pair check: |int_0^tau rho e^{i omega t} dt|^2 = F/omega^2 is finite as omega -> 0: F/omega^2 at omega = 1e-8 is 1.0 (-> tau^2 = 1)

